{
  "plan": {
    "operation": "change_assumption",
    "parent_shortcut": "fixed_environment",
    "response_change": {
      "type": "interaction_emergence",
      "parent_relation": "The target mapping is dL_dt = interface_mobility * interfacial_tension / (L + interface_width), so at fixed interface_mobility and interface_width it is a single monotone 1/(L+const) law with no dependence on the coarsening history beyond the separately relaxing auxiliary states.",
      "child_relation": "Release the assumption that interfacial tension is fixed by allowing the existing interfacial_tension parameter to evolve during aging, so the target depends on the interaction between instantaneous domain size and a time-relaxing capillary driving force, not only on mobility and diffuse-width relaxation.",
      "affected_inputs": [
        "L",
        "interfacial_tension"
      ],
      "observable_effect": "Across conditions with different capillary-aging timescales or residual tensions, the coarsening rate shows an additional early-to-late suppression or recovery at the same L that cannot be produced by the parent's single fixed tension."
    },
    "diagnostic_groups": [
      {
        "name": "capillary_drive",
        "expression": "interface_mobility*interfacial_tension/(L*(1 + interface_width/L))",
        "role": "Instantaneous curvature-driven coarsening law currently scored for the target"
      },
      {
        "name": "size_to_width",
        "expression": "L/interface_width",
        "role": "Diffuse-interface crossover coordinate separating early small-domain suppression from late sharp-interface behavior"
      },
      {
        "name": "mobility_tension_product",
        "expression": "interface_mobility*interfacial_tension",
        "role": "Net kinetic-capillary prefactor controlling the rate amplitude at fixed L and interface_width"
      }
    ],
    "activation_condition": "Observable when capillary aging occurs on a timescale comparable to the measured early-to-intermediate coarsening window, so interfacial_tension changes appreciably before L becomes asymptotically large; most visible when L is only a few times interface_width and mobility aging alone does not explain the amplitude change.",
    "train_hidden_contrast": "In training, the mechanism appears as systematic rate-amplitude drift beyond what the parent's relaxing mobility and width can capture; in holdout, the same capillary-aging interaction persists under different relaxation times or residual values, changing dL_dt at matched L without changing the observation protocol.",
    "continuation_path": "Recover the parent by taking interfacial_tension constant in time, equivalently a vanishing tension-relaxation rate or zero gap between initial and residual tension.",
    "freedom_change": "parameter_to_state",
    "promoted_symbol": "interfacial_tension",
    "promotion_reason": "The omitted mechanism is aging of the capillary driving force itself. Existing states only modulate kinetic mobility and diffuse-width correction; keeping interfacial_tension fixed cannot represent a history-dependent capillary amplitude change in the scored target mapping.",
    "gap_statement": "The current model treats the capillary driving force as time-independent even though interface structure can sharpen or chemically/structurally relax after the quench, changing the effective large-scale interfacial tension during the same window where mobility and width evolve.",
    "baseline_assumption": "Interfacial tension is constant.",
    "scientific_mechanism": "Post-quench interfacial free-energy relaxation causes the effective interfacial tension to evolve toward a late-time value, modifying the curvature-driven force during coarsening.",
    "affected_symbols": [
      "L",
      "interfacial_tension"
    ],
    "rationale": "A realistic next refinement is to relax the parent's fixed-interfacial-tension assumption rather than adding an unrelated force. The parent target law is effectively a fixed-environment amplitude times a size-dependent denominator: after accounting for interface_mobility(t) and interface_width(t), the dependence of dL_dt on L still uses one shared capillary scale interfacial_tension for all times and conditions. The scientific gap is that quenched interfaces often continue to structurally or chemically relax, which changes the free-energy cost per unit area and therefore the curvature driving force itself. Vary the capillary-aging condition through the relaxation timescale or residual tension while holding the initial conditions of L, interface_mobility, and interface_width fixed. Then two trajectories can pass through similar L and similar diffuse-width regime L/interface_width, yet have different dL_dt because the driving tension has evolved differently. A parent with one shared fixed interfacial_tension cannot reproduce that matched-L rate contrast across those conditions; it can only rescale all times uniformly through one constant tension and cannot create condition-dependent history effects in the scored mapping. This is observable when tension relaxation is appreciable during the sampled window, especially for early and intermediate times before asymptotic sharp-interface coarsening washes out the difference. The mechanism remains admissible because positive evolving tension still gives nonnegative dL_dt, the rate remains decreasing with increasing L at fixed states, and free-energy dissipation is preserved."
  },
